  "reasoning_content": "We need answer user's request: \"Explain how a relational database executes a SQL query, from parsing and planning through indexing, joins, transactions, and returning rows. Write a detailed technical tutorial with concrete examples and tradeoffs, including common performance pitfalls. Use complete sentences and avoid repeating yourself.\"\n\nNeed produce detailed technical tutorial. Need complete sentences, avoid repeating. Need cover parsing, planning, indexing, joins, transactions, returning rows. Concrete examples, tradeoffs, common pitfalls. We can structure. Need likely include SQL example, schema, EXPLAIN, access paths, cost model, MVCC, locking, isolation, index types, join algorithms (NL, hash, merge), statistics, cardinality, pushdown, subqueries, views, etc. Transactions and query execution: snapshot, locks, concurrency control, ACID, MVCC. Maybe mention read-only vs write. Need avoid repeating. Ensure complete sentences.\n\nNeed final only. Let's craft thorough tutorial. Need not too terse. Include examples with SQL and pseudo EXPLAIN. Discuss tradeoffs. Let's plan.\n\nTitle: How a relational database executes a SQL query.\n\nSections:\n1. What a database must do: compile SQL into physical plan, access data under concurrency.\n2. Setup schema and query.\nExample: orders, customers, products, order_items. Query:\nSELECT c.customer_name, p.product_name, oi.quantity, oi.unit_price\nFROM order_items oi\nJOIN orders o ON o.order_id = oi.order_id\nJOIN customers c ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id\nJOIN products p ON p.product_id = oi.product_id\nWHERE o.status = 'shipped' AND o.shipped_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\nORDER BY o.shipped_date, c.customer_name\nLIMIT 20;\n\nMaybe also query with subquery.\n3. Parsing and validation: lexer/parser to AST, name resolution, type checking, catalog, authorization, rewrite (common table expressions, view expansion, predicate pushdown). Mention parse errors, schema changes.\n4. Optimization/planning: logical optimization (predicate pushdown, join reordering, aggregation pushdown, subquery decorrelation), statistics (row count, NDV, histograms, correlation, skew), cost model (CPU, IO, memory, disk seeks, cache), choose physical plan. Use EXPLAIN. Discuss dynamic/static, prepared statements, parameter sniffing.\n5. Access paths/indexes: cluster/secondary",
